<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- When creating a new activity, make a copy of this file with appropriate name -->
<worksheet xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Interpretations of the Derivative</title>
  <!-- Optional introduction -->
  <objectives>
          <ul>
                  <li>
          <p>
                  The learner will use context and units to interpret the meaning of the derivative in practical settings and use the derivative at a point to estimate the value of the (original) function at nearby points.
          </p>
                  </li>
          </ul>
  </objectives>
  <introduction>
    <p>
Recall that for a function <m>f(t)</m>, the derivative at the point <m>t=a</m> is defined by 
            <md>
                    <mrow>f'(a) \amp =\lim_{b\to{}a}\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}</mrow>
                    <mrow>  \text{or equivalently}</mrow>
                    <mrow>f'(a) \amp=\lim_{h\to{}0}\frac{f(a+h)-f(a)}{(a+h)-a}</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp=\lim_{h\to{}0}\frac{f(a+h)-f(a)}{h}.</mrow>
            </md>
Noting that <m>f(b)-f(a)=\Delta{}f</m> is the change in output and <m>b-a=\Delta{}t</m> is the change in
the input variable, we can think of the derivative as 
<md>
        <mrow>f'(a)\amp=\lim_{\Delta{}t\to{}0}\frac{\Delta{}f}{\Delta{}t}</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp=\frac{\di{f}}{\di{t}},</mrow>
</md>
and the <em>units</em> of the derivative will be the units of the output of the function divided by the units
of the input to the function.
</p>
<p>
For example, with the function <m>s(t)=\left(64-16(t-1)^{2}  \right)\text{ ft}</m> that takes time
<m>t</m> in seconds as input and gives height in feet as its output, we have
<md>
        <mrow>s'(a) \amp=\lim_{b\to{}a}\frac{\left(s(b)-s(a) \right)\text{ ft}}{\left(b-a \right)\text{ s}}</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp=\left(-32a+32 \right)\text{ ft}/\text{s}.</mrow>
</md>
</p>
<p>
We also know that we can use numerical approximations, so for a given (small, finite) value of
<m>\Delta{}t</m>, 
<md>
        <mrow>f'(a)\amp\approx \frac{\Delta{}f}{\Delta{}t}</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp=\frac{f(a+\Delta{}t)-f(a)}{\Delta{}t}</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp=\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}.</mrow>
</md>
But, because <m>f'(a)\approx{}\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}</m>, we also have <m>f(b)-f(a)\approx{}f'(a)(b-a)</m>.
In other words, we can approximate the change in <m>f</m> from the change in <m>t</m> along with the
derivative!
</p><p>
In the following scenarios, use the context and the units to help you interpret the meaning of the
derivative of the appropriate functions, and use the derivative to estimate the value of the
original function at nearby points, as appropriate.
    </p>
  </introduction>
